+# Hotbar: Figma → Roblox notes
+
+Source: `design/figma/hotbar.json`, frame **HUD / Desktop** (`31:100`) and the **Hotbar Slot**
+component set (`31:2`). The code is in `src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/UI/Hotbar.luau`.
+
+The design is a 1920×1080 frame. Our UI is laid out in offsets for a screen 1080 pixels tall and
+`Screen.uiScale()` scales it up from there, so Figma pixel values are used directly.
+
+## What Roblox can't reproduce exactly
+
+| Design | Problem in Roblox | What we used instead |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Selected Name** font: Gilroy ExtraBold (800) | Gilroy isn't a Roblox font, and Roblox doesn't let you upload custom fonts. | **Montserrat ExtraBold**, the closest geometric sans Roblox has. It's a little wider than Gilroy, so names take up a bit more width. If the designer would rather match the rest of our UI, use `BuilderSans` ExtraBold instead. |
+| **Selected Name** letter spacing −0.5px | TextLabels have no letter-spacing (tracking) setting, and RichText doesn't support it either. | Default spacing. Names are about 0.5px per character wider than in the design. |
+| **Selected Name** line height 28px | `LineHeight` is a multiplier, not a pixel value. | The name is always one line, so the label is just 28px tall with the text centred vertically. That looks the same. |
+| **Selected Name** 2px outside stroke, black at 60% | `TextStrokeColor3` only draws a thin, fixed-width outline. | A `UIStroke` (Contextual) on the label: thickness 2, black, transparency 0.4. It draws outward from the glyph edges, but the corners are rounded differently from Figma's, so sharp letter corners look slightly softer. |
+| **Hotbar** layer opacity 0.8 | Roblox has no opacity for a frame and everything inside it. Setting transparency on each element separately isn't the same, because overlapping layers (a swatch on a slot on the bar) would show through each other. | A **`CanvasGroup`** with `GroupTransparency = 0.2`. This fades the bar as one layer, like Figma does. Downsides: a CanvasGroup renders to a texture, so it uses extra GPU memory and can look slightly soft at some scales. If that becomes a problem, swap it for a Frame with `BackgroundTransparency = 0.2` and keep the slots opaque. That's a visible change, so check with the designer first. |
+| **Hotbar** stroke: 2px inside, top edge only, white at 12% | `UIStroke` draws on all sides or none. | A 2px white `Frame` (transparency 0.88) across the top of the bar. The CanvasGroup's rounded corners clip it. In Figma the top stroke curves down into the corners and tapers off. Ours is a straight line cut off where the corners start, so the ends are slightly shorter and don't curve. |
+| **Hotbar Slot** corner smoothing 0.6 (squircle) | `UICorner` only draws circular arcs. Roblox has no corner smoothing. | `UICorner` 12px. The corners look a little rounder and less "iOS" than in Figma. Getting an exact squircle would need 9-slice images, and then the swatch colour couldn't be a simple fill. |
+| **Selected** stroke: 3px outside, horizontal linear gradient #FFD25A → #FF801A | A `UIStroke` can take a `UIGradient` as a child, but the gradient is stretched across the stroke's own bounds (slot plus 3px on each side), not just the slot. | `UIStroke` (Border mode, which draws outside) with a `UIGradient` child, rotation 0. The ends of the gradient sit about 3px further out than in Figma, which you can't see at this size. |
+| **Pressed** variant is 60×60 (swatch 36×36, badge at 3,3) | Not a Roblox limitation, but swapping a 64px slot for a 60px one inside the auto-layout bar would push every other slot over by 4px. | Each slot keeps a fixed 64×64 cell. A `Body` frame inside it shrinks to 60×60 around its centre, with a 36×36 swatch and the badge at 3,3. Click/tap detection uses an invisible 64×64 button on top, so a press near the edge still counts after the body shrinks. |
+| **Hover / Pressed** pointer states | Roblox has no variants. Touch screens have no hover. | The slot follows the hit area's `GuiState` (Idle → Default, Hover → Hover, Press → Pressed). On touch screens you get Pressed while your finger is down and never Hover, which is what you'd expect. |
+| **Selected combined with Hover/Pressed** | The design treats the four states as separate and doesn't show what a selected slot looks like when hovered or pressed. | The gradient stroke shows whenever the slot is selected. The fill and size still follow the pointer state, so a selected slot also darkens and shrinks when pressed. |
+
+## Matched exactly
+
+- Bar fill `#0E1020`, corner 14, padding 10, slot gap 8. That makes the bar 732×84, the same as in Figma.
+- Bar sits 24px above the bottom of the screen, with the name 8px above the bar.
+- Slot fills: Default `#262B45`, Hover `#313858`, Pressed `#1D2136`. Slots are 64×64 with corner 12.
+- Swatch 40×40, centred, corner 8. Its fill is the block's colour from `BlockTypes` (the design says to do this).
+- Key badge 18×18 circle, black at 55%, inset 4px. The key number is Builder Sans Bold 12, white, centred.
+- Selected Name: 22px, white, centred, width fits the text.
+
+## Behaviour (unchanged)
+
+- Clicking or tapping a slot selects that block (`Activated` on the hit area → `BlockPlacer.setBlockType`).
+- Number keys 1–9 and 0 still change the selection. `BlockPlacer` handles them, and the hotbar shows whatever is selected.
+- The design's `Show Key` property is always true. Every slot gets a badge as long as there's a key label for its position.
